1
00:00:01,060 --> 00:00:18,188
Okay, so this is our CDBG public meeting, hosted by the Office of Planning, Development and Sustainability. I'm Ashley Williams, I manage the CDBG program. And we also have Alicia Hunt, who's the director of the office.

2
00:00:20,062 --> 00:00:43,568
So the purpose of this meeting, usually we have one of these meetings in the spring, but we're starting to have one at this time of year as well. We're going to discuss some of the program's accomplishments that we've had, as well as the current use of the funding. And then, you know, we're still looking for that input for future programs or activities.

3
00:00:47,382 --> 00:01:06,333
Just for anybody on here that isn't familiar with the CDBG program, this program has been going on since 1974. It's a program of HUD, the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development. The primary benefit of this is to benefit low and moderate income persons.

4
00:01:06,721 --> 00:01:27,629
So Medford receives this grant from HUD and for those low moderate income persons, they have to be residents of Medford. It's a flexible annual grant and uses include public facilities, parks, public service agencies, which are usually nonprofits, housing and economic development.

5
00:01:28,034 --> 00:01:56,992
Any activities or projects need to show that they benefit at least 51% of low to moderate income persons. And that low to moderate income person is 80% or less of the area median income. So I know this map is a little tough to see on the screen. It is on our webpage on the city's website where you can kind of blow it up, but these kind of orange shaded areas are the low income block groups per the census.

6
00:01:57,346 --> 00:02:20,060
And if we look over on the chart, any of these shaded boxes fall into the low to moderate income definition for HUD for this current year. So if you're a single household, two family, three family, and so on, you have to be under this low 80%.

7
00:02:24,177 --> 00:02:52,392
Just our timeline. So the actual program year or the fiscal year starts July 1st of every year and ends on June 30th. So usually around January or February, we open the request for proposals for the public service applications. So it would be January or February of 2023 would be the next round. And that would be for services beginning July 1st of 2023.

8
00:02:52,898 --> 00:03:13,756
In April, we'll be working on our draft annual action plan, which will be available for comment. In May, we have a public hearing with the city council. Our office will request the city council to authorize the mayor to submit that one-year action plan to HUD. The actual plan is due on May 15.

9
00:03:14,887 --> 00:03:42,477
And then, so the program year will end on June 30th, and then on September 30th is when we have what we call the CAPER due. So that's what we're working on now, and it's the Consolidated Annual Performance and Evaluation Report. So just an easy way of saying we're listing all of our accomplishments and our performance for the year and evaluating what we did with the funding. So these are some of our accomplishments we've done this last program year.

10
00:03:44,317 --> 00:04:06,828
public facility improvements. These are usually our larger scale projects. They included roadway, sidewalk, utility improvements at Prescott, Alston, and Cottage Streets. There were various ADA improvements at Tufts Pool. It included some upgrades in the bathrooms for handicap access, as well as some new stairs going into the pool.

11
00:04:07,807 --> 00:04:22,758
We've started design work at Morrison Logan parks, and there was a few upgrades at the Medford fire stations engines three and five. These included new kitchens upgrades to LED lighting, a new roof.

12
00:04:26,572 --> 00:04:47,362
affordable housing. So there's a draft housing production plan complete. It identifies obstacles and opportunities to develop affordable housing in Medford. And then there's recommendations to overcome these obstacles. And that draft plan is currently pending approval by the Council and State Department of Housing and Community Development.

13
00:04:48,037 --> 00:05:16,370
Um, our housing staff also worked on creating more affordable units through private partnership with developers. This would be considered a leverage activity because it wouldn't really be any CDBG funds in it. And we've also increased funding and awareness for the home programs down payment assistant loans. There's actually a couple loans that were paid by the home consortium, which we are a member of for affordable

14
00:05:16,673 --> 00:05:41,952
home ownership units in Medford this year. And we've also, we get this question a lot, but a home rehab program, we are working on looking into nonprofits to assist with creating and overseeing this program. There just currently isn't the staffing to manage something like that, but we realize it is a need and we're looking for a way to have that program open again.

15
00:05:46,627 --> 00:06:16,478
public services. So this funding, we get 15% of our annual grant is allocated to public services. There's legal aid, elderly services, afterschool care, counseling, social, recreational, just a few of these on the screen. There's funds going to the Council on Aging, Communitas, Housing Families. This upcoming year, well, this current year now, we funded

16
00:06:16,630 --> 00:06:18,790
some programs at ABCD.

17
00:06:23,482 --> 00:06:51,123
economic development. So the city is creating new small business support programs, such as business counseling and facade improvement grant program. So currently, these are not being funded with CDBG. That was an original possibility. But there had been some other funding available that was decided to be utilized. But in the future, it will be considered to start funding some of these programs with CDBG funds as well.

18
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:14,140
So these are some of our upcoming projects. There's going to be some street and sidewalk improvements at Pinker Street, ADA compliance upgrades at some of the parks throughout the city. Recently there was a community garden being built of a praise village.

19
00:07:14,140 --> 00:07:36,331
possibly some more beds at that garden as well in the upcoming year. Morrison Park specifically, upgrades to the top lot, fencing and dugouts, and Logan Park, looking into a natural play area and a new pathway around, and then some new curbing and edges at Brooks Park.

20
00:07:40,094 --> 00:08:05,137
So this meeting is just to elicit any input, you know, comments, suggestions, questions, what are the needs for low to moderate income residents in Medford? Where should we be focusing our CDBG resources? And what do you think any of the priorities should be out of parks, public facilities, infrastructure, housing, economic development and public services?

21
00:08:17,928 --> 00:08:31,293
